        "Three broad systems run in parallel across Pakistani schooling, and most families interact with only one or two of them directly. Government and many lower-cost private schools follow the Matric and FSc (Intermediate) system set by provincial boards, examined domestically and central to admission into most public universities and medical or engineering colleges by default. English-medium private schools, particularly in the larger cities, run Cambridge O and A Level instead, examined externally by Cambridge International on its own May/June and October/November calendar. The IB Diploma sits as a third, much smaller option, offered at a limited number of schools generally positioning themselves around international university placement.",
